abstract: >
  This study extends the TEP-GNSS analysis to 25.3 years (2000-2025) using CODE analysis center data, encompassing 165.2 million station pair measurements. The temporal extension confirms the stability of originally detected signatures over decadal timescales and leverages the extended baseline to uncover long-period geophysical phenomena. Seven independent signatures converge: (1) Spatial anisotropy (EW:NS=2.16, strength=1.981, p<10⁻¹⁵), (2) orbital velocity coupling (r=-0.888, p<2×10⁻⁷, 5.1σ; 5M surrogates), (3) CMB frame alignment (r=0.747, p<0.001) definitively rejecting galactic motion (5,570× variance ratio; multi-resolution verification across 65,341 directions), (4) 56/156 planetary events significant (≥2σ; 25 Bonferroni-corrected), (5) 18.6-year lunar nutation (R²=0.641, p<10⁻⁸), (6) semiannual nutation (R²=0.904, p<10⁻²⁰), (7) network coherence (score=0.582). All core phenomenology from the multi-center study replicates over 25 years with enhanced statistical power.
